{"text":"The Router routine can't chain triggers — `fire_trigger` isn't available in standalone routine sessions. The fix is to move the routing into the Cloudflare Worker, which fires the correct trigger directly. Update your Worker code:

```javascript
const GUEST_TRIGGER_ID = "trig_01FBStZ7zidD8V1UUptqnVNS";
const BOOKING_TRIGGER_ID = "trig_0117u3BwuBofYTdWwaz8E2YV";

export default {
  async fetch(request, env) {
    if (request.method !== 'POST') return new Response('Method not allowed', { status: 405 });

    const body = await request.text();
    let payload;
    try { payload = JSON.parse(body); } catch { payload = {}; }

    const messages = payload.messages || [];
    const hasGuestMsg = messages.some(m => m.source !== "host");
    const triggerId = hasGuestMsg ? GUEST_TRIGGER_ID : BOOKING_TRIGGER_ID;

    const resp = await fetch(
      `https://api.anthropic.com/v1/claude_code/routines/${triggerId}/fire`,
      {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
          'Authorization': `Bearer ${env.CLAUDE_TRIGGER_TOKEN}`,
          'anthropic-beta': 'experimental-cc-routine-2026-04-01',
          'anthropic-version': '2023-06-01',
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        },
        body: JSON.stringify({ text: body }),
      }
    );
    return new Response(await resp.text(), { status: resp.status });
  }
};
```

The `CLAUDE_TRIGGER_TOKEN` secret needs to work for both downstream triggers — use whichever token you created for them (they share the same account, so one token should cover both). You no longer need the Router trigger at all.

